1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,562
Which infrastructure changes improve bike service, and does a simplified model make the same planning

2
00:00:06,562 --> 00:00:07,000
choice?

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,455
We start with the existing network. The original seven-bridge graph introduces topology, but ordinary bike

4
00:00:12,455 --> 00:00:15,000
trips do not require an Euler tour.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,200
Now enable the fixed northern bicycle link. Purple shows proposed infrastructure. The baseline and intervention

6
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:27,000
share exactly the same demand tape, bicycles, and operating policy.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,964
The ranking uses twelve matched seeds, while the maps show one saved episode. This link

8
00:00:33,964 --> 00:00:40,000
adds about zero point five eight completed trips on average, with substantial variation.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,769
Select a journey that actually uses the new connection. Blue marks its baseline path; purple

10
00:00:45,769 --> 00:00:50,000
marks its intervention path. The moving dots follow recorded route timing.

11
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,357
The selected origin station has a linked inventory trace. A shorter journey can change return

12
00:00:55,357 --> 00:01:00,000
timing, then change which later riders find a bicycle or an empty dock.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Change routing resolution to two hundred metres. The left map remains detailed; the right uses

14
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:12,000
the reduced network. Both contain the same proposed infrastructure.

15
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,724
For this candidate, the mean benefit changes sign, from plus zero point five eight to

16
00:01:18,724 --> 00:01:25,000
minus zero point five eight trips. The highest mean now belongs to another candidate.

17
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:32,800
At fourteen hundred metres the distortion is much larger. The reduction preserves directed route connectivity

18
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:38,000
but sends some trips through retained hubs, producing long detours.

19
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:45,200
The maps and completion curves expose the consequences. Washington mean route-length error is about sixty-nine

20
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:50,000
percent at this resolution. Poor simplifications remain in the comparison.

21
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,455
In Kaliningrad, stations and demand are hypothetical. With a cargo crew, the western link adds

22
00:01:55,455 --> 00:02:00,909
about one point nine two trips with detailed routing, but zero point one seven at

23
00:02:00,909 --> 00:02:02,000
two hundred metres.

24
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:09,222
The highest-mean intervention changes here too. Another fixed candidate is unused by these station routes

25
00:02:09,222 --> 00:02:15,000
and produces zero benefit. We do not substitute a more successful candidate.

26
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:23,333
Reopening an existing crossing is shown separately, against its disrupted baseline. This is recovery, not

27
00:02:23,333 --> 00:02:30,000
investment. Extra completions mean serving fixed requests, not generating new real-world demand.

28
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,759
The observed Washington workload covers seven April days, not months. Recorded rentals omit denied demand,

29
00:02:37,759 --> 00:02:45,000
and many benefits are tied. The whiskers describe variation across runs, not confidence intervals.

30
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,667
New-scenario mode calculates a matched pair with changed closure timing and inventory. Pending settings never

31
00:02:51,667 --> 00:02:53,000
reuse old trajectories.

32
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Original results remain auditable. These small, variable effects are sensitivity evidence, not reliable construction recommendations.
